hi it's michelle from lab muffin beauty  science chemistry phd skincare nerd  and skin care user we talk a lot about  how to use your products but today i  wanted to talk about how  not to use your products so i'm going to  go through some of the mistakes i see  people making when  using their products and why you  shouldn't do that  if you like talking nerdy about beauty  click the like the subscribe and the  notification bell so you don't miss any  videos  first the dropper on the face i see this  a lot on social media and i guess it  looks really cool  but the issue is that droppers aren't  meant to be used that way  and what i mean by that is the  preservative system inside the product  isn't designed to let you use it that  way so preservatives are put into a  product to stop  microbes like bacteria and fungi from  growing too much  if you have too much bacteria and fungi  in there you can end up with really  nasty infections when you put that  product back on your face  the way that they test a preservative  system is by mixing the product with  fungi of bacteria  and then waiting and seeing how much  fungi and bacteria grow  a good preservative system will actually  decrease the amount of fungi and  bacteria will actually kill anything  that's been added  with a bad preservative system the  bacteria and fungi might just  not change or they might even be allowed  to grow  with challenge testing the fungi or  bacteria actually gets reintroduced so  part way through the testing they chuck  in a bit more so this is meant to  simulate what would happen  if you dug into a jar with a dirty  finger or you touched a wand to your  skin or lips and it wasn't completely  clean  if the product passes this challenge  testing that means that you can put it  in a riskier type of packaging so like a  jar  or a product that has an applicator wand  and in general products want to minimize  the amount of preservatives they use  they want to put in just enough to do  the job preservatives tend to be  irritating and allergenic if you have  too much  they are some of the more irritating and  allergenic ingredients that you find in  cosmetics  so if you're touching the dropper to  your face you're making the preservative  system  do something that it wasn't meant to do  it's not in its job description  the second mistake is saving up the  serum from your sheet masks  this is a similar issue again you're  making the preservative system do a job  that it wasn't meant to do  single-use foil sachets are considered  one of the safest types of packaging  they're meant to be single used there's  no expected shelf life  after you open it there's not even air  going into it like there is in a tube  where  you dispense them and then a little bit  of air gets sucked back in  the preservative systems for these tend  to be the weakest so if you're saving up  the extra source  and keeping it for a week or so then  again you're making it do a job that it  wasn't meant to do  it is possible that the preservative  system can stand up to this  but really that product was only ever  intended to be used a few hours after  opening not  a few days or a week or a month after  you've opened up the sachet of the air  then there are lots of micros in the air  and cosmetics are really good food for  microbes  it's watery there's lots of nutrients  for them to grow sheet masks are usually  at a nice skin friendly and  micro friendly ph you're also probably  keeping it at room temperature which is  warm enough for these microbes to  usually reproduce really quickly and so  you are risking contamination  again with the bacteria talk getting  moisture into your products  water is really good for microbial  growth if you have a cosmetic product  then generally you will need to have a  preservative if it's got water in it  if you want it to last for longer than  like a week if there's extra water going  into a product then this can lead to  trouble  the worst situation is if there's a  product that has a big opening like a  hair mask in a jar  that you take into the shower and water  literally drips into it  if you're adding extra water a lot of  the time this sits on top  and so this doesn't get enough  preservative that extra water will be a  really good place for bacteria and  microbes to start growing  even if you just start using a product  in a damp bathroom  after you showered there might be enough  moisture there to cause problems so for  example if you get condensation inside  the container  inside like the walls of a jar for  example then  bacteria and microbes can start growing  there so if you want to avoid this water  contamination if you go into the shower  then it's a good idea to scoop out the  product and put it into a little  container like  one of these caps that you get from a  whole bunch of different products  if you're putting on skincare in the  bathroom let your bathroom air out and  dry out a bit before you start doing  your skincare routine  or you can use them in a different room  maybe your bedroom instead  transferring your products into a  different container i've been guilty of  this one in the past  it can be okay but you have to be really  careful  firstly the container has to be very  clean the preservative system in the  product might not be designed to  deal with whatever dust or bits of  cardboard or whatever's inside the  container  some containers are also incompatible  with certain products  glass is generally okay but plastics can  react with preservatives  or active ingredients or different  solvents inside the product  sunscreen ingredients for example can  soak into plastics and so that means you  have less of the sunscreen ingredients  in your sunscreen  they can also just dissolve the  container so if you have  a really clean container if you're only  putting the product in there for a very  short amount of time  or if you know that that container was  designed for that purpose  then it can be okay you can also test  the container with the product so you  can smear it on and look for visible  changes  but sometimes the changes aren't that  obvious so  just keep that in mind when you're using  that product not  applying enough sunscreen i see a little  goes a long way  all the time in sunscreen reviews the  problem is sunscreen is like  paint you are trying to hide your skin  from the sun  sunscreen is tested at two milligrams  per square centimeter to get that spf  label on the bottle  you can apply less sunscreen but the spf  will generally go down linearly  so that means if you apply half the  amount then you get about half the spf  but you run into more issues if you  apply less than half the amount  so your skin isn't flat it's  microscopically bumpy there are lots of  hills and valleys  so when you apply the sunscreen you fill  in the valleys first and if you don't  have enough sunscreen then you might not  be covering the tops of the heels of  your skin enough  and i think a lot of people do run into  this issue because there are studies  showing that most people  apply less than half the required amount  now the amount that you need to apply  does depend on how big your face is  but a general rule that covers pretty  much everyone is a quarter teaspoon for  your face  not including your ears or neck mixing  sunscreen with other products  it's really tempting to mix a sunscreen  with other products before you put it on  some sunscreens have really bad white  casts so mixing it with say  foundation makes it look a bit less  white when you put it on  the texture of some sunscreens also just  isn't great and so that way you can  dilute the texture and make it a bit  more wearable  some of us are lazy and we just want to  have one step  the problem is when you mix the  sunscreen with something else  then it can interfere with the film  formation when a sunscreen is tested  it's tested on its own on bare skin  and it's really important that it forms  a continuous film so that the sunscreen  can be  spread out evenly so you get even  protection if you add other stuff in  there it can mess up the microscopic  structure of the sunscreen  sunscreens are emulsions they're a bit  like mayonnaise if you add too much oil  or too much vinegar or too much  lecithin or whatever you can actually  break that emulsion  the mayonnaise will separate and same  with sunscreens  if it separates then you're not getting  that even layer so you shouldn't be  mixing sunscreens with well  anything so this is things like  foundations pigment drops and oils  so if you want other products on your  face as well then  apply it in layers in general it's a  good idea to wait five to ten minutes  after you've put on your sunscreen to  put on extra layers like foundation  it's also a good idea to find a  sunscreen that already does what you  want  so if you want to avoid a white cast  then maybe look for a chemical sunscreen  if you only want to do one step then  maybe look for a tinted sunscreen that  isn't too tinted so you can still apply  the right amount  if you want a product that's more  moisturizing then look for a sunscreen  that already has moisturizing  ingredients on it  or look for a moisturizer with spf  leaving  sunscreen in the heat with sunscreens  it's important to have the filters  distributed evenly through the whole  product  it's a bit like paint the pigment needs  to be evenly distributed  if it's settled out then you're not  going to get even coverage it's the same  idea with sunscreens  sunscreen should be formulated so that  the filters are spread evenly in the  product throughout the whole shelf life  but it does depend on storage heat is  something that's really good at making  sunscreens separate  so if you go to the beach and you keep  the bottle in the sun  that's not good if you keep your  sunscreen in the car  you put it in the glove box and you  leave your car parked in the sun  that's also not good i know it seems  like a good idea to leave a bottle of  sunscreen in the car so you don't forget  it  but because of the heat this is usually  not a great idea  so a better idea would be to keep your  sunscreen in your handbag  where it tends to have cooler  temperatures if you're going to the  beach  cover up your sunscreen with something  that reflects light so maybe a white  towel  even better keep it in a cooler bag also  pay attention to the expiry date  if you're not very good at keeping your  sunscreen out of the heat then make sure  you buy a new one every summer  derma rolling over products that aren't  meant to be derma rolled over  so with a derma roller you're opening up  channels in your skin  to let skincare products go deeper and  if they're not meant to go deeper then  this can be bad  some derma rollers have shorter needles  and these don't go as deep into your  skin so they tend to be a bit safer  but most products are designed to sit on  the surface of your skin  your skin is a good barrier so a lot of  the time the products have a higher  concentration  so that the amount that gets through the  barrier is still effective  so you can think of it like a slow  release but if you've got these extra  channels in your skin from the needles  then it's like a shortcut it's like it  suddenly all gets released at once  it's a bit like if you have an icy drink  if you drink it slowly it's really nice  but if you drink it too fast you get  brain freeze  or it's like pepper if it's evenly  distributed in your food and you're  eating it slowly you're getting a little  bit at once it's really nice  but if you eat a giant lump all at once  it's not great  i am really into analogies today so the  skin equivalent of this  is irritation the other thing that can  happen is again with the microbes if you  have a product that wasn't designed to  go into your skin  maybe the preservative system wasn't  designed to be quite as good  you could be introducing microbes deeper  into your skin leading to an infection  so these are some of the mistakes i've  been seeing around have you done any of  these  i've definitely done a few are there any  mistakes that i've missed let me know in  the comments  if you like this video click the thumbs  up and the subscribe you can also check  out my blog and follow me on instagram  as well see you next time for more  beauty science

